Thank you for your effort</span></span></div><div role="gridcell" aria-colindex="1" class="_ak8i"><span class=""></span><span class=""></span><span class=""></span></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="x10l6tqk xh8yej3 x1g42fcv" role="row" style="z-index: 1; transition: none; height: 76px; transform: translateY(152px);"><div class="x9f619 x4f6e3x x9orja2 xyri2b xohmsfg xzm8p2n"><span class="x140p0ai x1gufx9m x1s928wv xhkezso x1gmr53x x1cpjm7i x1fgarty x1943h6x x193iq5w xeuugli x13faqbe x1vvkbs x1lliihq x1fj9vlw xhslqc4 x1hx0egp x1f6kntn xjb2p0i x8r4c90 xo1l8bm x1ic7a3i x12xpedu" style="--x-fontSize: 14px; --x-lineHeight: 9.9531px; --x-8dd7yt: -0.0137em; --x-hxtmnb: 0.0137em;">Messages</span></div></div><div class="x10l6tqk xh8yej3 x1g42fcv" role="row" style="z-index: 0; transition: none; height: 76px; transform: translateY(228px);"><div role="gridcell" class="x1n2onr6" tabindex="0"><div tabindex="-1" class=""><div class="_ak72 false false false _ak73 _ak7d _asiw _ap1- _ap1_"><div class="_ak8n"></div><div class="_ak8l _ap1_"><div role="gridcell" aria-colindex="2" class="_ak8o"><div class="_ak8q"><div class="x1c4vz4f x3nfvp2 xuce83p x1bft6iq x1i7k8ik xq9mrsl x6s0dn4"><div class="x78zum5"><span dir="auto" title="Kinjal USA" class="x1iyjqo2 x6ikm8r x10wlt62 x1n2onr6 xlyipyv xuxw1ft x1rg5ohu _ao3e" style="min-height: 0px;">Kinjal USA</span></div></div></div><div class="_ak8i false"><span class="x140p0ai x1gufx9m x1s928wv xhkezso x1gmr53x x1cpjm7i x1fgarty x1943h6x x193iq5w xeuugli x13faqbe x1vvkbs x1lliihq x1fj9vlw xhslqc4 x1hx0egp x1pg5gke xjb2p0i xo1l8bm xl2ypbo x1ic7a3i" style="--x-fontSize: 12px; --x-lineHeight: 8.5313px; --x-8dd7yt: -0.0137em; --x-hxtmnb: 0.0137em;">2/22/2025</span></div></div><div class="_ak8j"><div class="_ak8k"><span class="x78zum5 x1cy8zhl" title="‪MEMORY

Memory is one of the brain’s most essential functions—it’s what lets us store, retain, and recall information as we navigate life. Without it, learning new things or adapting to changes would be nearly impossible. 

More than just a mental notebook, memory is deeply tied to who we are. Ourr experiences, knowledge, and even our sense of identity depend on it. The brain organizes memory into different systems that work together, making sure we can process, retrieve, and use information when we need it most.

&quot;Hands-on learning: The brain’s first tool.&quot;

From the moment we’re born, our hands are our first gateway to understanding the world. Babies explore by grabbing, touching, and manipulating objects, laying the groundwork for how they process information.

This isn’t just instinct —it’s the building block of cognitive development. As we grow, our hands continue to play a crucial role in learning and making sense of our surroundings.

**The lost art of using our hands**

In a world where keyboards and touchscreens rule, hands-on tasks like writing and creating have been pushed aside. But research supports what many teachers have long suspected:  **writing by hand strengthens memory more than typing.**


---The perks of keeping your hands busy---

There are plenty of reasons to pick up a hands-on hobby. Here are just a few:

1. Boosts brainpower in older adults – Helps improve cognitive, emotional, physical, and social skills, which can lead to greater self-esteem and a stronger sense of control over daily life.
2. Eases stress and anxiety – The repetitive, focused nature of these activities has a calming effect, making it a natural way to unwind and relax.
3. Good for the heart – When the mind is at ease, blood pressure can drop, which supports better cardiovascular health.
4. Helps manage chronic pain – Certain activities can reduce pain levels, making them especially helpful for people dealing with arthritis or osteoarthritis. No matter the arthritis or carpal tunnel syndrome, keeping your hands active (despite slight pain) is better than leaving them still for hours on end and letting musculature atrophy.
5. A natural mood booster – Engaging in crafts or needlework brings a sense of accomplishment, triggering the release of feel-good hormones like serotonin and oxytocin.
6. Improves mobility – The repetitive motions involved in many of these tasks can enhance coordination and flexibility, keeping hands and fingers nimble.‬"><div class="x78zum5 x1okw0bk xhslqc4 x16dsc37 x1xp8n7a xl56j7k x16q7b9a"><span aria-hidden="true" data-icon="status-dblcheck" class=""><svg viewBox="0 0 18 18" height="18" width="18" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" class="" version="1.1" x="0px" y="0px" enable-background="new 0 0 18 18"><title>status-dblcheck</title><path fill="currentColor" d="M17.394,5.035l-0.57-0.444c-0.188-0.147-0.462-0.113-0.609,0.076l-6.39,8.198 c-0.147,0.188-0.406,0.206-0.577,0.039l-0.427-0.388c-0.171-0.167-0.431-0.15-0.578,0.038L7.792,13.13 c-0.147,0.188-0.128,0.478,0.043,0.645l1.575,1.51c0.171,0.167,0.43,0.149,0.577-0.039l7.483-9.602 C17.616,5.456,17.582,5.182,17.394,5.035z M12.502,5.035l-0.57-0.444c-0.188-0.147-0.462-0.113-0.609,0.076l-6.39,8.198 c-0.147,0.188-0.406,0.206-0.577,0.039l-2.614-2.556c-0.171-0.167-0.447-0.164-0.614,0.007l-0.505,0.516 c-0.167,0.171-0.164,0.447,0.007,0.614l3.887,3.8c0.171,0.167,0.43,0.149,0.577-0.039l7.483-9.602 C12.724,5.456,12.69,5.182,12.502,5.035z"></path></svg></span></div><span dir="ltr" class="x1iyjqo2 x6ikm8r x10wlt62 x1n2onr6 xlyipyv xuxw1ft x1rg5ohu _ao3e" style="min-height: 0px;">MEMORY

Memory is one of the brain’s most essential functions—it’s what lets us store, retain, and recall information as we navigate life. Without it, learning new things or adapting to changes would be nearly impossible.&nbsp;

More than just a mental notebook, memory is deeply tied to who we are. Ourr experiences, knowledge, and even our sense of identity depend on it. The brain organizes memory into different systems that work together, making sure we can process, retrieve, and use information when we need it most.

"Hands-on learning: The brain’s first tool."

From the moment we’re born, our hands are our first gateway to understanding the world. Babies explore by grabbing, touching, and manipulating objects, laying the groundwork for how they process information.

This isn’t just instinct —it’s the building block of cognitive development. As we grow, our hands continue to play a crucial role in learning and making sense of our surroundings.

<strong class="_ao3e">*The lost art of using our hands</strong>*

In a world where keyboards and touchscreens rule, hands-on <span class="matched-text _ao3e">task</span>s like writing and creating have been pushed aside. But research supports what many teachers have long suspected:&nbsp;&nbsp;<strong class="_ao3e">*writing by hand strengthens memory more than typing.</strong>*


---The perks of keeping your hands busy---

There are plenty of reasons to pick up a hands-on hobby. Here are just a few:

1. Boosts brainpower in older adults – Helps improve cognitive, emotional, physical, and social skills, which can lead to greater self-esteem and a stronger sense of control over daily life.
2. Eases stress and anxiety – The repetitive, focused nature of these activities has a calming effect, making it a natural way to unwind and relax.
3. Good for the heart – When the mind is at ease, blood pressure can drop, which supports better cardiovascular health.
4. Helps manage chronic pain – Certain activities can reduce pain levels, making them especially helpful for people dealing with arthritis or osteoarthritis. No matter the arthritis or carpal tunnel syndrome, keeping your hands active (despite slight pain) is better than leaving them still for hours on end and letting musculature atrophy.
5. A natural mood booster – Engaging in crafts or needlework brings a sense of accomplishment, triggering the release of feel-good hormones like serotonin and oxytocin.
6. Improves mobility – The repetitive motions involved in many of these <span class="matched-text _ao3e">task</span>s can enhance coordination and flexibility, keeping hands and fingers nimble.</span></span></div><div role="gridcell" aria-colindex="1" class="_ak8i"><span class=""></span><span class=""></span></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="x9f619 x1n2onr6 xupqr0c x5yr21d x6ikm8r x10wlt62 x17dzmu4 x1i1dayz x2ipvbc xjdofhw xyyilfv x1iyjqo2"><div id="main" class="_ajx_ x1q80dvb"><div class="x10l6tqk x13vifvy x1o0tod xh8yej3 x5yr21d x182nak8 x1wwuglj x1vs56c6" data-asset-chat-background-beige="true" style="opacity: 0.1;"></div><header class="x1n2onr6 x9f619 x78zum5 x6s0dn4 xh8yej3 x7j6532 x1j6awrg x1te75w5 x1iygr5g x4eaejv xcock1l xl9llhp x1qj619r x1r9ni5o xb1pvdl x42zw1d x889kno x1a8lsjc xf7dkkf xv54qhq x1s928wv x1h1zc6f x1280gxy xstzfhl x11iowje"><div class="x78zum5 x6s0dn4 x1iyjqo2 xeuugli"><div class="x1n2onr6 x1c4vz4f x2lah0s xdl72j9 x1cbfl22 x1y332i5" title="Profile details" role="button"><div class="x1n2onr6 x1c9tyrk xeusxvb x1pahc9y x1ertn4p" style="height: 40px; width: 40px;"><div class="x10l6tqk x13vifvy x1o0tod x78zum5 x6s0dn4 xl56j7k xh8yej3 x5yr21d"><span aria-hidden="true" data-icon="default-group-refreshed" class=""><svg viewBox="0 0 48 48" height="212" width="212" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" class="xh8yej3 x5yr21d x1c9tyrk xeusxvb x1pahc9y x1ertn4p x4u6w88 x1g40iwv x140zg0z" fill="none"><title>default-group-refreshed</title><path fill-rule="evenodd" clip-rule="evenodd" d="M17.822 21.678Q19.143 23 21 23t3.178-1.322T25.5 18.5t-1.322-3.178Q22.857 14 21 14t-3.178 1.322T16.5 18.5t1.322 3.178M12.66 32.34q.66.66 1.589.661h13.5q.928 0 1.59-.66.66-.662.66-1.59v-.9q0-.956-.492-1.758A3.3 3.3 0 0 0 28.2 26.87a16.7 16.7 0 0 0-3.544-1.308q-1.8-.435-3.656-.436-1.856 0-3.656.436T13.8 26.869a3.3 3.3 0 0 0-1.308 1.223A3.3 3.3 0 0 0 12 29.85v.9q0 .928.66 1.59m21.09.66h-2.392A4.16 4.16 0 0 0 32 30.75v-.9c0-1-